---
import { getPublishedCollection } from "astro-course-university/content";
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// Read from the same collection /sessions/ renders, so this list can't say
// something different from the actual weekly schedule.
const sessions = (await getPublishedCollection("sessions")).sort((a, b) => a.data.week - b.data.week);
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Each week you build one small drawing program, then add exactly one new
    mechanism to it — a line that follows a pointer, then reads how fast the
    pointer is moving, then obeys a rule you chose (say, it can never cross
    itself), then keeps changing on its own even when nobody is touching it
    (ink that fades unless you keep drawing), then answers to a second input
    device through the same code path. The point of every mechanism is the
    same: an <em>instrument</em> behaves differently depending on whose hands
    are on it, unlike a fixed <em>artwork</em> you just show someone — and
    everything in this course is marked as an instrument, not an artwork.
    Twelve weeks build toward two different things: <strong>First
    Instrument</strong>, one build judged on how well it holds to a single
    constraint you chose, and <strong>Exhibition Piece</strong>, that same
    build staged to run unattended in a room for a stranger, judged as a
    whole rather than against a single rule.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    You should already be comfortable writing event-driven code — reading a
    mouse, touch or keyboard event and reacting to it, on canvas or in the
    DOM. This course does not teach that. What's new here is treating input
    as a continuous signal rather than a one-off callback: reading speed and
    dwell out of a stream of pointer events, keeping state that drifts on its
    own between events, and getting the same logic to answer correctly to
    more than one kind of input device. It's for interaction designers,
    generative artists, and programmers who want a semester on making
    software feel right in a stranger's hands, not just work in your own.
  </p>

  <h2>Learning outcomes</h2>
  <p>By the end of the semester, you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Build a real-time program that draws in direct response to live pointer, touch, or keyboard input.</li>
    <li>Derive a higher-order signal — speed, dwell, acceleration — from a raw stream of input events, and use it to change what's drawn.</li>
    <li>Choose one deliberate constraint, enforce it in code, and demonstrate the specific difference removing it makes.</li>
    <li>Give a build internal state that keeps changing on its own over time, independent of whether anyone is providing input.</li>
    <li>Route two or more distinct input devices through one shared code path, instead of a separate branch per device.</li>
    <li>Use a seeded random source so the same input reliably reproduces the same output, while still varying meaningfully between seeds.</li>
    <li>Choose and defend one deliberate failure mode as a design decision, and explain how it differs from an actual bug.</li>
    <li>Turn a single fixed build into a small family of related outputs by exposing its internal choices as parameters.</li>
    <li>Stage a build to run unattended for a first-time viewer, with no verbal explanation.</li>
    <li>Give and act on peer feedback across a semester, and show a specific decision that changed as a result.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Twelve weeks at a glance</h2>
  <ol>
    {
      sessions.map((session) => (
        <li>
          <a href={withBase(`/sessions/${session.id}/`)}>{session.data.title}</a>
          {session.data.description && <> — {session.data.description}</>}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching session.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
